# TalkReady – BACKEND Phased Prompts (Node.js + Express + MongoDB)

For the backend developer. Use these prompts one phase at a time with your AI coding assistant. Always attach `00_SHARED_API_CONTRACT.md` together with each prompt.

---

## HOW TO USE THIS FILE

1. Paste **PART A (Master Prompt)** once at the start of the session.
2. Then paste **one phase prompt at a time**, in order (Phase 0 → Phase 8).
3. After each phase the assistant must stop, give the report, and wait. Test the phase yourself (Postman) before starting the next one.
4. Never skip a phase: later phases depend on earlier ones.

Note: there is no existing code (no GitHub repository yet). This is a fresh project. The backend lives in the `backend/` folder of the `TalkReady` repository.

---

# PART A – MASTER PROMPT (paste once)

```
You are a Senior Node.js/Express/MongoDB backend engineer and API architect.

PROJECT: TalkReady – an offline-first mock interview application for students and job seekers. You are building ONLY the BACKEND (REST API). A different developer builds the React frontend on another laptop, so the API must match the attached API contract (00_SHARED_API_CONTRACT.md) EXACTLY: same paths, methods, field names, enums, status codes and response format.

TECH STACK (do not add other major libraries without telling me why):
- Node.js (LTS), Express.js, MongoDB, Mongoose
- Auth: JWT (jsonwebtoken) + bcryptjs
- Validation: express-validator (or zod – pick one and use it everywhere)
- Security: helmet, cors (configurable origins), express-rate-limit
- Uploads: multer; text extraction: pdf-parse (PDF) and mammoth (DOCX)
- PDF reports: pdfkit
- Logging: morgan (+ a small logger util)
- Tests: jest + supertest + mongodb-memory-server
- Config: dotenv

ARCHITECTURE: MVC with a service layer.
backend/
  config/ (db.js, env.js, constants.js)
  controllers/  (thin: read request, call service, send response)
  services/     (business logic: auth, scoring, question selection, report, resume parsing)
  models/       (Mongoose schemas)
  routes/       (one file per module + routes/index.js)
  middleware/   (auth, role, validate, error handler, upload, rate limit, notFound)
  validators/   (request validation per module)
  utils/        (ApiError, asyncHandler, response helpers, pagination helper)
  seeders/      (questions, categories, roles, skills, admin user)
  tests/
  uploads/      (gitignored, resume files)
  server.js, app.js (app separate from server so tests can import app)

NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES
1. Follow the API contract exactly. If something in the contract is unclear or wrong, STOP and ask me; do not invent a different API.
2. Every response uses { success, message, data } / { success:false, message, errors:[] }. Use shared helpers; never hand-write response objects in controllers.
3. All async handlers wrapped (asyncHandler); one central error handler. Never return stack traces to the client. Log them on the server only.
4. Every endpoint validates input and returns 400 with a field-level errors array.
5. Ownership: users can only read/change their own interviews, answers, reports, notes, resumes, settings. Another user's record returns 404.
6. Never return passwordHash, tokenVersion, JWT secret or internal fields. Use a toJSON transform: _id → id, remove __v.
7. Register static routes before dynamic ones (/questions/categories before /questions/:id, /reports/progress before /reports/:id).
8. Never hardcode categories, roles, skills or questions inside controllers. Enums come from config/constants.js; roles/skills/questions come from the database (seeded).
9. Core interview scoring must work WITHOUT any external AI service (deterministic rule-based). Label results with evaluation method "rule-based-v1". Never claim AI was used.
10. No secrets in code. Provide .env.example. .gitignore must include .env, node_modules, uploads/.
11. Do not add dummy/fake endpoints, empty controllers or TODO-only files. Every route file you create must be wired and working.
12. Do not claim something works unless you actually ran it. Do not fabricate test results. If you cannot run something, say so.

WORKING STYLE FOR EVERY PHASE
- Start by listing the files you will create/change and the endpoints covered.
- Implement the phase completely.
- Then run: npm install (if needed), the server, and the tests for this phase. Fix every error before reporting.
- Finish with a report: files created/modified, endpoints done, how to test (Postman examples), test results (real output), known issues.
- STOP after the report and wait for me to say "continue".

Reply "Ready" and wait for Phase 0.
```

